WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.208 --> 00:00:16.151
That afternoon Dr.

2
00:00:16.191 --> 00:00:17.612
Craven came to see Colin.

3
00:00:18.812 --> 00:00:20.893
He always visited after a temper tantrum.

4
00:00:22.193 --> 00:00:25.674
"Last night Mary flew at Colin like a cat," Mrs.

5
00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:28.215
Medlock explained as she led the doctor upstairs.

6
00:00:28.235 --> 00:00:32.416
"She stamped her feet and ordered him to stop screaming.

7
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.801
Somehow she startled him so that he stopped.

8
00:00:38.062 --> 00:00:41.463
And today well, you won't believe your eyes."

9
00:00:43.204 --> 00:00:47.005
In Colin's room he and Mary were talking and laughing over a book.

10
00:00:48.766 --> 00:00:52.308
But as soon as they noticed the adults, the two children grew silent.

11
00:00:52.328 --> 00:00:56.409
"I'm sorry to hear you're ill, my boy," said Dr.

12
00:00:56.449 --> 00:00:59.070
Craven. "I'm much better now.

13
00:01:00.271 --> 00:01:03.272
I'm going out in my wheelchair soon, if the weather is good.

14
00:01:04.616 --> 00:01:05.677
I want some fresh air."

15
00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:09.058
Dr. Craven sat down next to him.

16
00:01:09.078 --> 00:01:11.779
"I thought you didn't like fresh air."

17
00:01:11.799 --> 00:01:16.261
"I don't when I'm by myself, but Mary is coming with me."

18
00:01:16.281 --> 00:01:19.182
"And so is your nurse, of course."

19
00:01:20.463 --> 00:01:22.224
"No, I won't need the nurse."

20
00:01:23.504 --> 00:01:25.425
Colin sounded like a rajah again.

21
00:01:25.445 --> 00:01:28.706
"I'm always better when Mary is with me.

22
00:01:29.927 --> 00:01:32.388
A very strong boy I know will push my wheelchair."

23
00:01:34.132 --> 00:01:36.114
Dr. Craven felt alarmed for a moment.

24
00:01:37.515 --> 00:01:42.339
If Colin got well, then the doctor would lose all chance of inheriting Misselthwaite Manor.

25
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:43.960
But Dr.

26
00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.301
Craven was not a bad man.

27
00:01:46.682 --> 00:01:48.704
He wouldn't let Colin run into danger.

28
00:01:48.724 --> 00:01:52.967
"This boy who's pushing you must be strong," said Dr.

29
00:01:52.987 --> 00:01:56.070
Craven. "And I must know something about him.

30
00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:57.011
Who is he?"

31
00:01:57.031 --> 00:02:00.453
"It's Dickon," said Mary.

32
00:02:01.694 --> 00:02:04.577
She was sure that everyone knew Dickon, and she was right.

33
00:02:04.597 --> 00:02:08.320
"Oh, you'll be safe with him," said Dr.

34
00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:11.544
Craven. "He's as strong as a moor pony."

35
00:02:13.265 --> 00:02:14.606
That afternoon Dr.

36
00:02:14.647 --> 00:02:17.890
Craven did not give any medicine or leave instructions for the nurse.

37
00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:22.394
"This certainly is a new state of things," he said to Mrs.

38
00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:24.176
Medlock as they went downstairs.

39
00:02:25.537 --> 00:02:28.480
That night Colin slept peacefully for the first time.

40
00:02:29.836 --> 00:02:33.919
He had been awake for only ten minutes the next morning, when Mary ran into his room.

41
00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:37.162
"There's that nice smell again!"

42
00:02:37.602 --> 00:02:38.303
cried Colin.

43
00:02:38.323 --> 00:02:40.545
"You've been outside!"

44
00:02:40.565 --> 00:02:44.868
"You never saw anything so beautiful," said Mary.

45
00:02:44.888 --> 00:02:47.930
"Spring has really come!"

46
00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:49.732
"Has it?"

47
00:02:50.152 --> 00:02:51.313
Colin sat up in bed.

48
00:02:51.333 --> 00:02:53.355
"Open the window."

49
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.822
When Mary opened the window, fresh smells and birdsongs came pouring into the room.

50
00:02:59.842 --> 00:03:02.984
"Lie on your back and breathe deeply.

51
00:03:04.124 --> 00:03:05.225
That's what Dickon does."

52
00:03:06.665 --> 00:03:08.086
Colin did as he was told.

53
00:03:09.406 --> 00:03:14.169
He lay still and breathed in the fresh air while Mary described everything she had seen in the

54
00:03:14.229 --> 00:03:15.069
garden that morning.

55
00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:21.332
She also told him how Dickon had rescued a newborn lamb whose mother had died.

56
00:03:22.788 --> 00:03:25.589
After a while the nurse brought breakfast for Colin and Mary.

57
00:03:25.609 --> 00:03:29.191
"When do you think Dickon will come?"

58
00:03:29.651 --> 00:03:30.311
asked Colin.

59
00:03:31.572 --> 00:03:33.292
"Soon," replied Mary.

60
00:03:34.633 --> 00:03:37.334
And in a few minutes, they heard a crow cawing.

61
00:03:37.354 --> 00:03:41.696
"That's Soot," said Mary.

62
00:03:41.716 --> 00:03:45.157
"Listen. Do you hear a tiny sound?"

63
00:03:45.177 --> 00:03:47.198
"Oh, yes!"

64
00:03:47.738 --> 00:03:48.478
cried Colin.

65
00:03:49.759 --> 00:03:50.659
"That's the lamb.

66
00:03:51.079 --> 00:03:51.980
Dickon's coming."

67
00:03:53.704 --> 00:04:00.109
In a moment Martha opened the door and announced, "If you please, sir, here's Dickon and his creatures."

68
00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.872
Dickon entered the room, wearing his nicest smile.

69
00:04:06.113 --> 00:04:10.136
The newborn lamb was in his arms, and the fox trotted by his side.

70
00:04:11.477 --> 00:04:14.079
Nut sat on one shoulder and Soot on the other.

71
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.421
Shell's head peeped out of his coat pocket.

72
00:04:18.742 --> 00:04:20.584
Colin sat up slowly and stared.

73
00:04:22.016 --> 00:04:23.757
He had never seen anyone like this.

74
00:04:25.038 --> 00:04:26.178
He didn't know what to say.

75
00:04:27.819 --> 00:04:31.001
But Colin's behavior did not make Dickon feel shy or awkward.

76
00:04:32.182 --> 00:04:34.884
Wild creatures always stared at him until they knew him.

77
00:04:36.144 --> 00:04:39.486
He walked over to the sofa and laid the lamb on Colin's lap.

78
00:04:40.767 --> 00:04:43.669
The lamb began to butt its head against Colin's side.

79
00:04:43.689 --> 00:04:45.730
"What is it doing?"

80
00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:46.830
asked Colin.

81
00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:49.352
"It wants its mother.

82
00:04:50.524 --> 00:04:53.586
I brought it to you a bit hungry because I knew you'd like to see it feed."

83
00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:58.010
Dickon knelt by the sofa and took a bottle out of his pocket.

84
00:04:59.371 --> 00:05:04.935
Colin watched in amazement while Dickon fed the lamb and told them how he'd discovered it on the

85
00:05:04.975 --> 00:05:08.898
moor. "I heard a weak cry, but couldn't find it.

86
00:05:10.158 --> 00:05:12.760
At last I spotted a bit of white near a rock."

87
00:05:14.542 --> 00:05:17.464
As Dickon talked, Soot flew in and out of the window.

88
00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:23.663
Nut and Shell made trips to the big trees outside and ran up and down the branches.

89
00:05:24.984 --> 00:05:28.146
Captain curled up near Dickon after the lamb fell asleep.

90
00:05:29.487 --> 00:05:33.629
The children looked at some of Colin's books, which were full of pictures of flowers.

91
00:05:33.649 --> 00:05:40.434
"We call that one columbine," said Dickon, pointing to a flower that had a long Latin name in

92
00:05:40.474 --> 00:05:40.814
the book.

93
00:05:40.834 --> 00:05:44.116
"There are big clumps of columbine in the garden.

94
00:05:45.413 --> 00:05:48.697
They'll look like a bed of blue and white butterflies when they're blooming."

95
00:05:48.717 --> 00:05:52.262
"I'm going to see them," Colin said firmly.

96
00:05:53.584 --> 00:05:54.765
Mary nodded solemnly.

97
00:05:54.785 --> 00:05:58.571
"Yes. Yes, you are," she said.
